# Resolution on a Ceiling for Landholdings
Tamil title: நில உச்ச வரம்புத் தீர்மானம்

Speech delivered in the Legislative Assembly on 20 October 1958.

Mr Speaker, I am genuinely pleased that the Finance Minister says he has not departed from the principle of imposing a ceiling upon landholdings. However clearly and sincerely he spoke at the All India Congress Committee, a widespread impression arose in the country. Some welcomed what they read; others worriedly asked when our Finance Minister had become counsel for the mirasidars.

We should commend him for saying today that neither he nor the Government has abandoned the policy. What surprises me is that every argument he advanced today is also heard at mirasidars' conferences. I do not understand how the Government's case for a ceiling and the case of those determined not to surrender existing land can sound alike. Perhaps great minds meet when they reach the summit.

Mirasidars too say in principle that a ceiling may be desirable but insist that it must be approached cautiously. The Finance Minister similarly warns that immediate and definite implementation may harm production. Since the arguments resemble each other, people have reason to suspect that he has leaned toward the landlords.

Most people cannot analyse ideal, explanation, plan and implementation as elaborately as the Finance Minister. They lack time. They simply hear that when a land-ceiling resolution was proposed, the Finance Minister said it should not be accepted, and they ask why. He has the strength to endure public discussion, and I recognise that strength.

**C. Subramaniam:** The resolution there concerned food production, not the land question. N. K. Palanisami suggested that production would automatically rise if land were divided and distributed. I said that was mistaken and required careful examination. No resolution on a ceiling came before us, and no occasion arose to oppose one.

**C. N. Annadurai:** The Minister clarifies that the issue there was food production, not a land ceiling. I mention it only so that his speech there does not prejudice the resolution now before us.

We have adopted the goal of a ceiling, and all members should thank him for assuring us that he has not slipped away from it. In explanation, he says he has long argued that good seed, irrigation, fertiliser and credit must be supplied to cultivators; merely dividing land will not increase production.

Those asking for land do not believe that, once it is distributed, Siva's attendants will descend and plough it as in the story of Nandanar. They ask in the confidence that they can then obtain good seed, fertiliser, grants and loans. Just as Government has a duty to distribute land to poor cultivators, it must help them turn that land into productive fields.

The Government may argue that it lacks facilities or has not framed sufficient plans. The Minister even invited us to charge it with that. We are not so great that we eagerly accuse him. My concern is that, though he understands the land-ceiling problem and has responsibility for addressing it, the proposed committee of legislators was not established earlier and put to work. Had it been, today's obstruction and hesitation might have been avoided.

If the Minister understands these problems and possesses the ability to solve them but leaves them unresolved, I must regretfully wonder whether, without realising it, he has become an instrument in the hands of their opponents.

No one argues that land should be distributed only to increase food production. Distribution serves another ideal: people should acquire a right in property and the conviction that the fruits of their labour belong to them. The socialist issue is that excessive wealth should not accumulate with one person. Socialism did not arise merely to raise food output.

Food production has increased both in capitalist America and in the Soviet Union, which stands as a pivot of socialism. When discussing planned production, we must also consider what share reaches those who perform the labour.

The Finance Minister spoke of Coimbatore's fertile land and able workers. I am pleased that one day allowed discussion of both Chengalpattu and Coimbatore.

**C. Subramaniam:** There are good workers there. That is the reason.

**M. Kalyanasundaram:** Does that mean the others are lazy?

**C. N. Annadurai:** The Minister should understand the full meaning of “virgin soil.” Production is high because the soil is fresh and the workers capable. The soil of our district has reached something like the age of Avvaiyar. Even with good labourers, Chengalpattu therefore receives little yield.

I know Coimbatore's land, having lived there for years, and know the remarkable ability of its cultivators. They clear difficult scrub and forest, prepare the ground and labour to make it fertile. Their enthusiasm grows because the land and what it yields are theirs. No merchant will carry it away for excessive profit. Grain returns to the household gruel pot, becomes gold jewellery for a beloved wife, adds six or seven thousand rupees to savings, or sometimes is spent by an unworthy child. The knowledge that the benefit remains with the family makes labour more productive.

The enthusiasm born of owning land exists neither in seed nor fertiliser. I do not say those inputs are unnecessary; Government must supply them. But it must also supply the encouragement created by the right to enjoy the fruit of one's own labour.

When the Minister asks what benefit comes from giving land to a person without facilities, he should remember that when land was granted in parcels of three or ten acres to Congress freedom fighters, the Government did not first ask whether they possessed ploughs, equipment, money, seed or fertiliser. It acted because they had sacrificed and honour could be gained by supporting them.

As the Minister says, some prospered through hard work, some fell into debt, and others passed the land to people they had intended to benefit. Misuse occurred in various ways. That experience cannot be used to escape all future distribution. I hoped the Minister would make that clear today, rather than allow large newspapers to print his portrait and proclaim, "Look, he says dividing land is wrong," flattering him into confusion.

Ministers must consider policy conflicts and practical difficulties encountered while implementing ideals. The Minister once cited holdings of one or two thousand acres under peasant proprietorship and asked in 1953 why production had not increased. In other speeches he now says production has increased. Why reuse today an argument once deployed to say subdivision would not improve output?

If food production and distribution truly need not be joined, there is no reason not to accept Kothandaraman's resolution. Its ideal is simply that land above a ceiling should be acquired and given to landless poor people. The Finance Minister should agree that accepting it need not damage production.

If he accepts it openly, people will understand the direction in which we travel. Government acceptance would display not only magnanimity but confidence in its ideal.

The Minister should issue a public clarification that the Government remains committed to imposing and implementing a land ceiling and that it is wrong to fuse that question with food-production policy. Otherwise people will continue saying, "The Finance Minister from agricultural Coimbatore has explained that a ceiling will harm food output."

We saw how firmly Karuthiruman resisted giving even a little more paddy to cultivators. When we asked for more, he asked why transplanting labourers should not receive it too, eventually extending the argument to cattle. Once a person has property rights, surrender is never easy. The sense of ownership does not disappear through argument or merely by joining a party. Leaders who think party membership transforms proprietors into people willing to relinquish land are mistaken. The ceiling exists precisely because ownership sentiment among landlords will not quickly decline.

**P. G. Karuthiruman:** Annadurai says landowners will not surrender property. Once a ceiling law is enacted, I announce that I am prepared to give my surplus land to whomever the honourable member names.

An intervening exchange about a person to whom land may already have been given is partly damaged in OCR.

**P. G. Karuthiruman:** I have given it to him. The honourable member does not know that.

**C. N. Annadurai:** After the law is enacted, many people who recognise the goodwill of good men may certainly receive land from people such as Karuthiruman.

Members ask what will happen to holdings above the ceiling and warn that food plans will suffer if the land goes uncultivated. We need not fear that. Devise means to distribute it among landless poor people. If cooperative cultivation is proposed, the Government says cooperatives have not produced good results.

When the Opposition states a worthy ideal, Government asks how it can implement it. It says it cannot operate cooperative cultivation properly, refuses the Opposition's goal, frames no good alternative, and will not consult Opposition members about implementation. In that condition, argument based merely upon the ruling party's numerical strength serves no purpose.

Experts capable of advising how surplus land should be divided, cultivated and kept productive certainly exist. Yet the Government's food-production committee contains ruling-party members and officials but no Opposition representative. The Minister says he is willing to hear ideas inside or outside the House, but the arrangement resembles a host who leaves a nighttime guest outside, eats alone, then returns and says, "Dinner is over; let us sleep and look after you in the morning." That may not be Coimbatore custom, though such customs exist in famine regions.

When we suggest schemes, the Finance Minister calls them slaughterhouse plans or poultry-and-egg plans and asks whether they will yield anything before ten years. I have raised chickens. A hen lays by the sixth month; unless one delays incubation, chicks hatch in about twenty-five days. We need not wait ten years.

**C. Subramaniam:** I said that, under present conditions, raising the scale enough to produce an income of three crore rupees would take ten years.

**C. N. Annadurai:** Proposals must be examined properly. It is easy to ridicule a plan. Those suggesting one cannot always deliver every detail; they can outline it. Treating outlines as entertainment will neither understand nor solve the problem. Given the opportunity, we too could mock the Minister's outlines and say he must be an excellent cultivator merely because he comes from Coimbatore. I do not believe he wishes to cultivate that habit. I speak at length because such ridicule should not enter democratic practice.

If individuals receiving productive land lack facilities, it may be entrusted to Panchayat Unions, with Government grants, and the resulting asset or yield made the Panchayat's property. There is no use paralysing ourselves by asking what to do about every possible complication. No worthwhile plan is free from difficulties.

The Minister knows that pleasure lies in resolving a knot. When he spins thread, how often it breaks, how often it must be joined, and what pleasure comes from tying the knot and drawing the thread onward. I speak in the confidence that he does spin.

Recognising that satisfaction lies in resolving complications, let him demonstrate his ability to remove them. I conclude by thanking him for clearly stating that imposing a land ceiling remains Government policy.

---NOTES---
The heading date is split and damaged across OCR lines, but the filename `nilaucha301058` and visible fragments support 20 October 1958. The source remains unchanged.

“Virgin soil” is printed as an English loan phrase in Tamil and is central to the exchange distinguishing natural fertility from labour quality. Anna's comparison of Chengalpattu soil with elderly Avvaiyar is retained as humour.

The phrase “peasant proprietor” and portions of the 1952/1953 production discussion are OCR-damaged. The translation follows the recoverable policy distinction without asserting uncertain figures.

One short exchange around Karuthiruman's earlier gift of land is incomplete in OCR and is marked rather than reconstructed.

The final spinning metaphor addresses policy complications as knots to be joined and worked through, not reasons to abandon the scheme.
